RITE PARENT BODIES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/809/1/6)\n  45. NATURE'S STARSHIPS. I. OBSERVED ABUNDANCES AND RELATIVE FREQUENCIES OF AMINO ACIDS IN METEORITES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/783/2/140)\n  46. Hypothesis Article A Thermodynamic Basis for Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis (https://www.mso.anu.edu.au/~aditya/psi_seminars/2011/Pudritz/Higgs_and_Pudritz_2009.pdf)\n  47. Spectroscopy of Hydrothermal Reactions 25: Kinetics of the Decarboxylation of Protein Amino Acids and the Effect of Side Chains on Hydrothermal Stability (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231633952_Spectroscopy_of_Hydrothermal_Reactions_25_Kinetics_of_the_Decarboxylation_of_Protein_Amino_Acids_and_the_Effect_of_Side_Chains_on_Hydrothermal_Stability)\n  48. The reaction kinetics of alanine and glycine under hydrothermal conditions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/223355391_The_reaction_kinetics_of_alanine_and_glycine_under_hydrothermal_conditions)\n  49. Hydrothermal Reactions of Biomolecules Relevant for Microalgae Liquefaction (https://pubs.acs.org/iecred/article/54/47/11733/949086/Hydrothermal-Reactions-of-Biomolecules-Relevant)\n  50. Thermal Stability of Threonine in the Presence of a Marine Polyphenolic Material (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.151.3707.214)\n  51. Radiolytic Decomposition and Radiation Sensitivity of Amino Acids in Aqueous Solution by Gamma Irradiation\\* (https://academic.oup.com/jrr/article/6/3-4/130/984246)\n  52. T- and pH-dependent OH radical reaction kinetics with glycine, alanine, serine, and threonine in the aqueous phase - Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics (RSC Publishing) (https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2022/cp/d1cp05186e)\n  53. Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H2S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1019191108)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Threonine is treated as a late, low-flux surface product rather than a hydrothermal metabolite. The network centers on two experimentally anchored nodes: cyanosulfidic access to lactaldehyde followed by Strecker aminonitrile formation and difficult hydrolysis, and Cu-assisted aldol coupling of glycine with acetaldehyde in concentrated pools. Glycine and acetaldehyde are explicit hubs because their supply is the main bottleneck, and all productive routes are treated as nonselective threonine/allothreonine mixtures. Direct H2S-rich spark and methanol-ice photolysis routes are retained only as trace alternative channels, while hydrothermal synthesis is omitted because threonine is unstable and not demonstrated under vent-like conditions.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid (allo diastereomer)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"allothreonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methanol\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile-mediated access to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm UV, phosphate-buffered water near pH 7, bisulfide present; this node compresses the explicit glycolonitrile substep\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde first captures cyanide as glycolonitrile, then Cu(I)/Cu(II) sulfide photoredox and hydrolysis furnish glycolaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This overall step follows the cyanosulfidic homologation chemistry of Ritson & Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The explicit glycolonitrile intermediate is collapsed here to keep the network compact while preserving the experimentally important glycolaldehyde node. The chemistry is experimentally grounded, but it depends on UV-driven sulfide photoredox and inherits concerns about sulfidic-anion availability and UV attenuation (Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic reduction of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm UV, phosphate-buffered water near pH 7, bisulfide present\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II)-mediated sulfide photoredox reduces the cyanide-derived C2 carbonyl network and shifts glycolaldehyde toward acetaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) place acetaldehyde downstream of glycolaldehyde reduction in the same HCN/H2S/UV network that yields sugar and amino-acid precursors. The threonine literature does not quantify this step as directly as the later lactonitrile to lactaldehyde conversion, so it remains somewhat underconstrained, but chemically it is essential because every credible threonine route needs a C2 carbonyl source.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonitrile formation from acetaldehyde and HCN\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN-rich water, neutral to mildly alkaline\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon of acetaldehyde to give the cyanohydrin lactonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson & Sutherland (2013) used acetaldehyde cyanohydrin, lactonitrile, as the substrate furnishing lactaldehyde. Cyanohydrin formation from aldehydes and HCN is straightforward and chemically better supported than the later hydrolysis to free amino acid. Cobb & Pudritz (2015) also identify 2-hydroxypropanal as threonine's Strecker aldehyde, making lactonitrile the proximate precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoredox conversion of lactonitrile to lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"254 nm UV, phosphate-buffered water near pH 7, bisulfide present; reported lactaldehyde yield about 19%\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II)-mediated sulfide photoredox converts the cyanohydrin network into the corresponding hydroxy aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is one of the few threonine-specific experimental results in the literature: lactonitrile furnished lactaldehyde in 19% yield under CuCN/H2S/UV conditions (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013). Because lactaldehyde supply is the rate-limiting carbonyl node for Strecker threonine, it is kept explicit in the network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactaldehyde Strecker aminonitrile formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"freshly generated lactaldehyde in cyanide- and ammonia-bearing water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lactaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, then cyanide adds to generate the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson & Sutherland (2013) reported that adding HCN and NH3 converted lactaldehyde smoothly to the threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile, and Patel et al. (2015) described such alpha-aminonitriles as inevitable by-products of cyanosulfidic chemistry. However, no aminonitrile yield or diastereomer ratio was reported, so this step is plausible but quantitatively underconstrained.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of threonine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cool pooled water or mild geothermal cycling; avoid prolonged heating above about 330K\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile hydration proceeds through amide-like intermediates to the free amino acids, preserving the nonenzymatic threonine/allothreonine mixture.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This hydrolysis is chemically required to turn the aminonitrile into free threonine, but it is also the largest evidence gap in the designed Strecker route: Ritson & Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015) stopped at the aminonitrile stage, with no reported amino-acid yield or threo:allo ratio. The node is therefore explicitly low-confidence and assigned to cool pooled-water conditions, because threonine decomposes rapidly at elevated temperature and is disfavored in hydrothermal settings (Aubrey et al., 2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Overall Strecker synthesis of glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"overall Strecker plus hydrolysis in cool surface water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, cyanide gives aminoacetonitrile, and subsequent hydrolysis yields glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This node compresses the better-established glycine Strecker sequence into a single overall step. Patel et al. (2015) explicitly treat glycine alpha-aminonitrile as an inevitable by-product of cyanosulfidic photochemistry, and glycine itself is abundantly observed in discharge and ice experiments. That makes this a much stronger prebiotic amino-acid node than threonine formation (Patel et al., 2015; Ring et al., 1972; Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al., 2002).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Akabori-type aldol synthesis of threonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(II)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mildly basic, bicarbonate-rich or evaporatively concentrated pool; high local glycine and acetaldehyde\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal chelation activates the glycine alpha-carbon toward addition to acetaldehyde, giving a beta-hydroxy-alpha-amino-acid mixture after proton transfers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the only intentionally optimized chemistry that reports free threonine in substantial amount: copper glycinate plus acetaldehyde gave up to 64% combined threonine and allothreonine, with modest and condition-dependent threo bias (Sato, Okawa & Akabori, 1957; Ikutani et al., 1959). It is included because it is chemically strong and uses glycine, the most abundant abiotic amino acid, but its prebiotic realism is limited by high Cu loading, base requirement, and reversibility toward retro-aldol cleavage. It should therefore be interpreted as a low-volume pool chemistry, not as a globally robust route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"H2S-rich spark discharge glycine and acetaldehyde pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K bulk, with transient plasma much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"sub-atmospheric to 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"electric discharge over CH4/N2/H2O with NH3 and H2S or CO2 admixtures; condensate later aged and hydrolyzed\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Electrical discharge generates radicals, HCN, carbonyls, and amino-nitrile precursors that hydrolyze to glycine while leaving an aldehyde-rich condensate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycine is strongly supported in discharge chemistry, and threonine appears only in chemically richer H2S-bearing runs (Ring et al., 1972; Weber & Miller, 1981; Parker et al., 2011). Acetaldehyde is not directly quantified in the cited threonine analyses, so its explicit inclusion here is an inferred but chemically reasonable carbonyl source; this node is therefore partially speculative and is used only as a feeder into better-defined aldol and Strecker chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine release from UV-irradiated methanol-bearing ice residues\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"10-80K irradiation, then 250-300K warm-up\",\n        \"pressure\": \"low during irradiation; approximately 1 atm after warm-up\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"H2O:CH3OH:NH3:CO:CO2 ice mixture; product release after warm-up and hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV photolysis of mixed ices builds complex refractory organics that release amino acids such as glycine upon warm-up and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Interstellar or surface ice photolysis experiments consistently yield glycine after hydrolysis, with glycine among the major products (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al., 2002; Nuevo et al., 2008). This provides a plausible exogenous or cryogenic source of glycine that can later enter warmer surface-pool chemistry. The reaction is written as an overall residue-to-glycine transformation rather than a stepwise ice network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct trace threonine formation in H2S-rich spark discharge\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K bulk, with transient plasma much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"sub-atmospheric to 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"electric discharge; best evidence from the H2S-rich archived Miller mixture\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Broad radical and nitrile chemistry in discharge condensates yields a complex amino-acid mixture in which threonine and allothreonine are minor hydrolysis products.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Threonine and allothreonine were detected as trace products in reducing spark chemistry, and Parker et al. (2011) found threonine specifically in Miller's archived H2S-rich 1958 experiment. Weber & Miller (1981) tabulated roughly equal threonine and allothreonine relative yields, consistent with a nonselective route through hydroxy-aldehyde Strecker chemistry (Ring et al., 1972; Weber & Miller, 1981; Parker et al., 2011). This is a demonstrated but very low-yield route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct trace threonine formation in UV-irradiated methanol-bearing ices\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"10-80K irradiation, then 250-300K warm-up\",\n        \"pressure\": \"low during irradiation; approximately 1 atm after warm-up\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"VUV or UV irradiation of H2O:CH3OH:NH3:CO:CO2 ice, then warm-up and hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photolysis of mixed ices builds complex organics; warm-up and hydrolysis release a trace amino-acid suite that occasionally includes threonine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nuevo et al. (2008) reported threonine in only a few UV-irradiated ice residues, and never as an abundant product; Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. (2002) had already shown that such ice residues can release amino acids after hydrolysis. This route is therefore included as a sporadic, low-flux source rather than a major synthesis channel.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic lactaldehyde Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and HCN enter a cyanosulfidic UV network that reaches glycolaldehyde, then acetaldehyde, then lactonitrile and lactaldehyde before aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis to threonine/allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker glycine plus cyanosulfidic acetaldehyde aldol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde-based Strecker chemistry supplies glycine, the cyanosulfidic branch supplies acetaldehyde, and a later Akabori-type aldol step yields threonine/allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-cyanosulfidic mixed aldol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A spark condensate supplies glycine, a cyanosulfidic branch supplies acetaldehyde, and concentrated-pool aldol chemistry converts the pair to threonine/allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Ice-cyanosulfidic mixed aldol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Ice photolysis residues provide glycine, the cyanosulfidic branch provides acetaldehyde, and later aldol coupling yields threonine/allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker-spark mixed aldol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker chemistry supplies glycine, and a spark condensate contributes an acetaldehyde-rich pool for later aldol coupling.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-pool self-coupled aldol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"A single spark condensate supplies both glycine and acetaldehyde, which later react in a Cu-assisted aldol step to give threonine/allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Ice-spark mixed aldol pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Ice-derived glycine combines with an aldehyde-rich spark condensate in a later aldol reaction to give threonine/allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark-fed lactaldehyde Strecker pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"An inferred spark-derived acetaldehyde pool is routed through lactonitrile, lactaldehyde, and the threonine aminonitrile before hydrolysis to threonine/allothreonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct H2S-rich spark pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Trace threonine and allothreonine emerge directly from an H2S-rich spark-discharge mixture after condensate hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct methanol-ice photolysis pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Trace threonine is released directly from UV-irradiated methanol-bearing ice residues after warm-up and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine", "overall_strategy": "Threonine is treated as a late, low-flux surface product rather than a hydrothermal metabolite. The network centers on two experimentally anchored nodes: cyanosulfidic access to lactaldehyde followed by Strecker aminonitrile formation and difficult hydrolysis, and Cu-assisted aldol coupling of glycine with acetaldehyde in concentrated pools. Glycine and acetaldehyde are explicit hubs because their supply is the main bottleneck, and all productive routes are treated as nonselective threonine/allothreonine mixtures. Direct H2S-rich spark and methanol-ice photolysis routes are retained only as trace alternative channels, while hydrothermal synthesis is omitted because threonine is unstable and not demonstrated under vent-like conditions.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "lactonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "lactaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "threonine aminonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid", "common_name": "threonine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid (allo diastereomer)", "common_name": "allothreonine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "CH4O", "iupac_name": "methanol", "common_name": "methanol", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Glycolonitrile-mediated access to glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuCN"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "CuCN", "aqueous": true, "other": "254 nm UV, phosphate-buffered water near pH 7, bisulfide present; this node compresses the explicit glycolonitrile substep"}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde first captures cyanide as glycolonitrile, then Cu(I)/Cu(II) sulfide photoredox and hydrolysis furnish glycolaldehyde.", "reasoning": "This overall step follows the cyanosulfidic homologation chemistry of Ritson & Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015). The explicit glycolonitrile intermediate is collapsed here to keep the network compact while preserving the experimentally important glycolaldehyde node. The chemistry is experimentally grounded, but it depends on UV-driven sulfide photoredox and inherits concerns about sulfidic-anion availability and UV attenuation (Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanosulfidic reduction of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuCN"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "CuCN", "aqueous": true, "other": "254 nm UV, phosphate-buffered water near pH 7, bisulfide present"}, "mechanism": "Cu(I)/Cu(II)-mediated sulfide photoredox reduces the cyanide-derived C2 carbonyl network and shifts glycolaldehyde toward acetaldehyde.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) place acetaldehyde downstream of glycolaldehyde reduction in the same HCN/H2S/UV network that yields sugar and amino-acid precursors. The threonine literature does not quantify this step as directly as the later lactonitrile to lactaldehyde conversion, so it remains somewhat underconstrained, but chemically it is essential because every credible threonine route needs a C2 carbonyl source."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Lactonitrile formation from acetaldehyde and HCN", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN-rich water, neutral to mildly alkaline"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon of acetaldehyde to give the cyanohydrin lactonitrile.", "reasoning": "Ritson & Sutherland (2013) used acetaldehyde cyanohydrin, lactonitrile, as the substrate furnishing lactaldehyde. Cyanohydrin formation from aldehydes and HCN is straightforward and chemically better supported than the later hydrolysis to free amino acid. Cobb & Pudritz (2015) also identify 2-hydroxypropanal as threonine's Strecker aldehyde, making lactonitrile the proximate precursor."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Photoredox conversion of lactonitrile to lactaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuCN"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "CuCN", "aqueous": true, "other": "254 nm UV, phosphate-buffered water near pH 7, bisulfide present; reported lactaldehyde yield about 19%"}, "mechanism": "Cu(I)/Cu(II)-mediated sulfide photoredox converts the cyanohydrin network into the corresponding hydroxy aldehyde.", "reasoning": "This is one of the few threonine-specific experimental results in the literature: lactonitrile furnished lactaldehyde in 19% yield under CuCN/H2S/UV conditions (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013). Because lactaldehyde supply is the rate-limiting carbonyl node for Strecker threonine, it is kept explicit in the network."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Lactaldehyde Strecker aminonitrile formation", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "freshly generated lactaldehyde in cyanide- and ammonia-bearing water"}, "mechanism": "Lactaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, then cyanide adds to generate the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "Ritson & Sutherland (2013) reported that adding HCN and NH3 converted lactaldehyde smoothly to the threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile, and Patel et al. (2015) described such alpha-aminonitriles as inevitable by-products of cyanosulfidic chemistry. However, no aminonitrile yield or diastereomer ratio was reported, so this step is plausible but quantitatively underconstrained."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrolysis of threonine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "290-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cool pooled water or mild geothermal cycling; avoid prolonged heating above about 330K"}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile hydration proceeds through amide-like intermediates to the free amino acids, preserving the nonenzymatic threonine/allothreonine mixture.", "reasoning": "This hydrolysis is chemically required to turn the aminonitrile into free threonine, but it is also the largest evidence gap in the designed Strecker route: Ritson & Sutherland (2013) and Patel et al. (2015) stopped at the aminonitrile stage, with no reported amino-acid yield or threo:allo ratio. The node is therefore explicitly low-confidence and assigned to cool pooled-water conditions, because threonine decomposes rapidly at elevated temperature and is disfavored in hydrothermal settings (Aubrey et al., 2009)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Overall Strecker synthesis of glycine", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_002", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "overall Strecker plus hydrolysis in cool surface water"}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, cyanide gives aminoacetonitrile, and subsequent hydrolysis yields glycine.", "reasoning": "This node compresses the better-established glycine Strecker sequence into a single overall step. Patel et al. (2015) explicitly treat glycine alpha-aminonitrile as an inevitable by-product of cyanosulfidic photochemistry, and glycine itself is abundantly observed in discharge and ice experiments. That makes this a much stronger prebiotic amino-acid node than threonine formation (Patel et al., 2015; Ring et al., 1972; Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al., 2002)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Akabori-type aldol synthesis of threonine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Cu(II)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(II)", "aqueous": true, "other": "mildly basic, bicarbonate-rich or evaporatively concentrated pool; high local glycine and acetaldehyde"}, "mechanism": "Metal chelation activates the glycine alpha-carbon toward addition to acetaldehyde, giving a beta-hydroxy-alpha-amino-acid mixture after proton transfers.", "reasoning": "This is the only intentionally optimized chemistry that reports free threonine in substantial amount: copper glycinate plus acetaldehyde gave up to 64% combined threonine and allothreonine, with modest and condition-dependent threo bias (Sato, Okawa & Akabori, 1957; Ikutani et al., 1959). It is included because it is chemically strong and uses glycine, the most abundant abiotic amino acid, but its prebiotic realism is limited by high Cu loading, base requirement, and reversibility toward retro-aldol cleavage. It should therefore be interpreted as a low-volume pool chemistry, not as a globally robust route."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "H2S-rich spark discharge glycine and acetaldehyde pool", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K bulk, with transient plasma much hotter", "pressure": "sub-atmospheric to 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "electric discharge over CH4/N2/H2O with NH3 and H2S or CO2 admixtures; condensate later aged and hydrolyzed"}, "mechanism": "Electrical discharge generates radicals, HCN, carbonyls, and amino-nitrile precursors that hydrolyze to glycine while leaving an aldehyde-rich condensate.", "reasoning": "Glycine is strongly supported in discharge chemistry, and threonine appears only in chemically richer H2S-bearing runs (Ring et al., 1972; Weber & Miller, 1981; Parker et al., 2011). Acetaldehyde is not directly quantified in the cited threonine analyses, so its explicit inclusion here is an inferred but chemically reasonable carbonyl source; this node is therefore partially speculative and is used only as a feeder into better-defined aldol and Strecker chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Glycine release from UV-irradiated methanol-bearing ice residues", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "10-80K irradiation, then 250-300K warm-up", "pressure": "low during irradiation; approximately 1 atm after warm-up", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "H2O:CH3OH:NH3:CO:CO2 ice mixture; product release after warm-up and hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "UV photolysis of mixed ices builds complex refractory organics that release amino acids such as glycine upon warm-up and hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Interstellar or surface ice photolysis experiments consistently yield glycine after hydrolysis, with glycine among the major products (Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al., 2002; Nuevo et al., 2008). This provides a plausible exogenous or cryogenic source of glycine that can later enter warmer surface-pool chemistry. The reaction is written as an overall residue-to-glycine transformation rather than a stepwise ice network."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Direct trace threonine formation in H2S-rich spark discharge", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350K bulk, with transient plasma much hotter", "pressure": "sub-atmospheric to 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "electric discharge; best evidence from the H2S-rich archived Miller mixture"}, "mechanism": "Broad radical and nitrile chemistry in discharge condensates yields a complex amino-acid mixture in which threonine and allothreonine are minor hydrolysis products.", "reasoning": "Threonine and allothreonine were detected as trace products in reducing spark chemistry, and Parker et al. (2011) found threonine specifically in Miller's archived H2S-rich 1958 experiment. Weber & Miller (1981) tabulated roughly equal threonine and allothreonine relative yields, consistent with a nonselective route through hydroxy-aldehyde Strecker chemistry (Ring et al., 1972; Weber & Miller, 1981; Parker et al., 2011). This is a demonstrated but very low-yield route."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Direct trace threonine formation in UV-irradiated methanol-bearing ices", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "10-80K irradiation, then 250-300K warm-up", "pressure": "low during irradiation; approximately 1 atm after warm-up", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "VUV or UV irradiation of H2O:CH3OH:NH3:CO:CO2 ice, then warm-up and hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "Photolysis of mixed ices builds complex organics; warm-up and hydrolysis release a trace amino-acid suite that occasionally includes threonine.", "reasoning": "Nuevo et al. (2008) reported threonine in only a few UV-irradiated ice residues, and never as an abundant product; Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. (2002) had already shown that such ice residues can release amino acids after hydrolysis. This route is therefore included as a sporadic, low-flux source rather than a major synthesis channel."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Cyanosulfidic lactaldehyde Strecker pathway", "description": "Formaldehyde and HCN enter a cyanosulfidic UV network that reaches glycolaldehyde, then acetaldehyde, then lactonitrile and lactaldehyde before aminonitrile formation and hydrolysis to threonine/allothreonine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_007", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Strecker glycine plus cyanosulfidic acetaldehyde aldol pathway", "description": "Formaldehyde-based Strecker chemistry supplies glycine, the cyanosulfidic branch supplies acetaldehyde, and a later Akabori-type aldol step yields threonine/allothreonine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Spark-cyanosulfidic mixed aldol pathway", "description": "A spark condensate supplies glycine, a cyanosulfidic branch supplies acetaldehyde, and concentrated-pool aldol chemistry converts the pair to threonine/allothreonine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Ice-cyanosulfidic mixed aldol pathway", "description": "Ice photolysis residues provide glycine, the cyanosulfidic branch provides acetaldehyde, and later aldol coupling yields threonine/allothreonine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Strecker-spark mixed aldol pathway", "description": "Formaldehyde Strecker chemistry supplies glycine, and a spark condensate contributes an acetaldehyde-rich pool for later aldol coupling.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Spark-pool self-coupled aldol pathway", "description": "A single spark condensate supplies both glycine and acetaldehyde, which later react in a Cu-assisted aldol step to give threonine/allothreonine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Ice-spark mixed aldol pathway", "description": "Ice-derived glycine combines with an aldehyde-rich spark condensate in a later aldol reaction to give threonine/allothreonine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_009", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_009", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Spark-fed lactaldehyde Strecker pathway", "description": "An inferred spark-derived acetaldehyde pool is routed through lactonitrile, lactaldehyde, and the threonine aminonitrile before hydrolysis to threonine/allothreonine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_009", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Direct 