RITE PARENT BODIES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/809/1/6)\n  45. NATURE'S STARSHIPS. I. OBSERVED ABUNDANCES AND RELATIVE FREQUENCIES OF AMINO ACIDS IN METEORITES - IOPscience (https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/0004-637X/783/2/140)\n  46. Hypothesis Article A Thermodynamic Basis for Prebiotic Amino Acid Synthesis (https://www.mso.anu.edu.au/~aditya/psi_seminars/2011/Pudritz/Higgs_and_Pudritz_2009.pdf)\n  47. Spectroscopy of Hydrothermal Reactions 25: Kinetics of the Decarboxylation of Protein Amino Acids and the Effect of Side Chains on Hydrothermal Stability (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231633952_Spectroscopy_of_Hydrothermal_Reactions_25_Kinetics_of_the_Decarboxylation_of_Protein_Amino_Acids_and_the_Effect_of_Side_Chains_on_Hydrothermal_Stability)\n  48. The reaction kinetics of alanine and glycine under hydrothermal conditions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/223355391_The_reaction_kinetics_of_alanine_and_glycine_under_hydrothermal_conditions)\n  49. Hydrothermal Reactions of Biomolecules Relevant for Microalgae Liquefaction (https://pubs.acs.org/iecred/article/54/47/11733/949086/Hydrothermal-Reactions-of-Biomolecules-Relevant)\n  50. Thermal Stability of Threonine in the Presence of a Marine Polyphenolic Material (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.151.3707.214)\n  51. Radiolytic Decomposition and Radiation Sensitivity of Amino Acids in Aqueous Solution by Gamma Irradiation\\* (https://academic.oup.com/jrr/article/6/3-4/130/984246)\n  52. T- and pH-dependent OH radical reaction kinetics with glycine, alanine, serine, and threonine in the aqueous phase - Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics (RSC Publishing) (https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2022/cp/d1cp05186e)\n  53. Primordial synthesis of amines and amino acids in a 1958 Miller H2S-rich spark discharge experiment (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1019191108)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.38\n- Feedback: The graph is connected and the overall strategy aligns reasonably well with the threonine literature: low-flux surface production, explicit acetaldehyde and glycine bottlenecks, nonselective threo and allo outcomes, and omission of hydrothermal synthesis are all sensible. However, the network is not viable as written because the deterministic balance check marks 10 of 12 reactions UNBALANCED; only rxn_003 is balanced as written, and rxn_008 is acceptable only if written with explicit 2:2 stoichiometry or split into separate product branches. The specific reactions needing correction are rxn_001, rxn_002, rxn_004, rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_007, rxn_009, rxn_010, rxn_011, and rxn_012. Key fixes are to make the cyanosulfidic sequence explicit with glycolonitrile or cyanohydrin intermediates and full H2S/Cu/UV bookkeeping; add H2O to rxn_005; rewrite rxn_006 as one aminonitrile plus water giving one amino acid plus NH3 with separate threo and allo branches; rewrite rxn_007 as explicit aminoacetonitrile formation plus hydrolysis; write rxn_008 as 2 glycine + 2 acetaldehyde -> threonine + allothreonine or as separate product reactions; and replace rxn_009-rxn_012 with multistep spark or ice source modules including the real feedstocks such as H2O, CO or CO2, H2S, and hydrolysis rather than one-step reactions from CH4 or CH3OH. Environmental labels also need tightening: spark discharge and cryogenic UV ice chemistry are not generic 'Surface' reactions, while rxn_006 and rxn_008 should be described as specific prebiotic pool or hydrolysis settings rather than 'Biochemical'. P2 is the best current pathway, but no pathway is close to approval.\n- Issues: [\"Conceptually this is the most literature-aligned dedicated threonine route, but 5 of 6 steps are conservation violations. rxn_001 should not convert CH2O + HCN directly to glycolaldehyde; add explicit glycolonitrile or cyanohydrin chemistry. rxn_002 and rxn_004 omit the H2S/Cu/UV redox bookkeeping needed for the cyanosulfidic branch. rxn_005 is essentially the correct Strecker step but needs H2O as a product. rxn_006 is the main failure: one aminonitrile cannot hydrolyze to both threonine and allothreonine, and the 'Biochemical' label does not specify a plausible prebiotic hydrolysis setting.\", \"This is the best current pathway because the glycine plus acetaldehyde hub is chemically sensible and literature-grounded, and rxn_008 corresponds to known Akabori-type chemistry. However, acetaldehyde supply depends on unbalanced rxn_001 and rxn_002, rxn_007 compresses Strecker formation and hydrolysis into an unbalanced overall step, and rxn_008 should be written as 2 glycine + 2 acetaldehyde -> threonine + allothreonine or as separate threo and allo branches. The final step also needs explicit Cu(II), base, and concentration conditions rather than the vague 'Biochemical' label.\", 'Mixed sourcing is plausible in principle, but rxn_009 is not a valid stoichiometric representation of H2S-rich spark chemistry: CH4 + NH3 cannot yield both glycine and acetaldehyde without additional carbon and oxygen feedstocks. Together with unbalanced rxn_001 and rxn_002, three of four steps are invalid, and the pathway definition is somewhat redundant because rxn_009 already supplies both hubs. Transfer from discharge products to an aldol-active pool is not justified.', 'Ice-derived glycine is literature-consistent only as a minor residue product after UV irradiation and hydrolysis, but rxn_010 overcompresses that chemistry; the balance check suggests missing CO on the reactant side and H2 as a product, and real experiments also require H2O, CO, and CO2. rxn_001 and rxn_002 still mis-specify acetaldehyde supply, and rxn_008 needs explicit Cu, base, and concentration conditions. A thaw or transport step from ice residue to surface pool is implied but not modeled.', 'The mixed Strecker and spark aldol idea is coherent, but rxn_007 is unbalanced and under-specifies the water and hydrolysis bookkeeping of glycine formation, while rxn_009 is chemically invalid as written. rxn_008 can serve as the target-forming step only if the pathway specifies concentrated, alkaline, Cu-assisted pool conditions. Because rxn_009 already co-produces glycine, the division of labor between the two precursor sources is also blurry.', 'This is the shortest non-direct route, but it depends almost entirely on rxn_009, which is not a conserved or mechanistic representation of spark chemistry. rxn_008 is the only chemically grounded step, and even it needs explicit 2:2 stoichiometry or separate product branches. As written, this pathway is better interpreted as a placeholder for observed spark product mixtures than as a specified synthesis route.', 'Both precursor-supply steps are overcompressed: rxn_010 under-specifies UV ice chemistry and rxn_009 under-specifies H2S-rich discharge chemistry. No physical route is given for merging the two product pools before rxn_008, and the final aldol step still lacks explicit Cu and base conditions and corrected stoichiometry. The pathway is conceptually possible but poorly specified.', 'Using spark chemistry only as an acetaldehyde feed is literature-aware, but rxn_009 is still impossible as written, rxn_004 omits the CN-loss and O-source chemistry needed to obtain lactaldehyde, rxn_005 needs H2O as a product, and rxn_006 is invalid because one aminonitrile cannot hydrolyze to both threonine and allothreonine. Environmental handoff from discharge to photoredox and Strecker conditions is not described.', \"Trace threonine and allothreonine have been reported in some H2S-rich spark residues, so the idea aligns with the literature at a phenomenological level, but rxn_011 is not a chemically valid reaction. CH4 + NH3 cannot conserve the atoms needed for two C4 amino acids, and the 'Surface' label does not capture atmospheric discharge plus post-hydrolysis workup. This should be modeled as an empirical mixture output, not a one-step synthesis.\", \"Minor literature support exists for threonine in UV-photolyzed methanol-bearing ices, but rxn_012 is not conserved and omits the real multicomponent ice matrix and hydrolysis step. CH3OH + NH3 alone cannot furnish a C4 amino acid. The 'Surface' label is also inaccurate for cryogenic UV ice chemistry. As written this is only a trace empirical placeholder, not a specified pathway.\"]\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Threonine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Threonine is treated as a late, low-flux product that should accumulate only in cool surface or prebiotic-pool settings. The network therefore uses the two best-supported threonine chemistries from the literature: a cyanosulfidic surface sequence that upgrades formaldehyde and HCN to lactaldehyde and then to a nonselective threonine aminonitrile pool, and a separate Cu-assisted aldol capture of independently available glycine by acetaldehyde. Ten pathways are represented as distinct precursor-entry routes through these two connected modules; allothreonine is kept explicit, while productive hydrothermal threonine synthesis is omitted because current evidence for it is poor and threonine is thermally fragile at high temperature.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile pool\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid (threo diastereomer)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"threonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid (allo diastereomer)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"allothreonine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation of glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"273-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic cyanide-rich evaporitic pool or shoreline film; near-neutral to mildly alkaline.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to formaldehyde to form the cyanohydrin glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolonitrile is the canonical HCN-formaldehyde adduct and a key entry point in cyanosulfidic prebiotic chemistry, linking simple C1 feedstocks to higher-carbon aldehydes and amino-acid precursors (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015). Making this node explicit avoids the overcompressed and unbalanced C1-to-C2 shortcuts criticized in earlier drafts.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"CuCN-sulfide photoredox reduction of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated sulfidic surface pool, roughly pH 7 with phosphate buffering; elemental sulfur is written as the net oxidized sulfur product.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu-mediated sulfide photoredox converts the nitrile-bearing precursor to the aldehyde, with hydrolytic loss of ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson & Sutherland demonstrated CuCN/H2S/UV reduction of cyanohydrin and nitrile pools toward carbonyl compounds, and Patel et al. embedded this logic in a wider cyanosulfidic network. Writing elemental sulfur explicitly keeps the redox bookkeeping balanced; real sulfur speciation may be more complex. Sulfidic anion availability is itself a known environmental limitation for this chemistry (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Net sulfide photoreduction of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated sulfidic pool; net sulfur oxidation is written as S(0); low-oxygen, phosphate-bearing surface setting.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfide-driven photoredox reduction/deoxygenation of the C2 hydroxyaldehyde yields acetaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. derive acetaldehyde from glycolaldehyde reduction within the cyanosulfidic system. This exact isolated step is less directly quantified than the lactonitrile-to-lactaldehyde conversion, so it should be treated as network-supported rather than individually high-confidence. It is nevertheless essential because both the Strecker and aldol routes to threonine depend on acetaldehyde supply (Patel et al., 2015; Ritson & Sutherland, 2013).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation of lactonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"273-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic cyanide-rich pool; near-neutral to mildly alkaline.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to acetaldehyde to form the cyanohydrin lactonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acetaldehyde cyanohydrin, lactonitrile, is the direct precursor that Ritson & Sutherland photoreduced to lactaldehyde. This is therefore the chemically required link between the acetaldehyde hub and threonine's Strecker aldehyde; Cobb & Pudritz also identify lactaldehyde as the threonine Strecker aldehyde in parent-body modeling (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Cobb & Pudritz, 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"CuCN-sulfide photoredox reduction of lactonitrile to lactaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV-irradiated sulfidic surface pool, roughly pH 7 with phosphate buffer; elemental sulfur is the net oxidized sulfur product.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu-sulfide photoredox converts lactonitrile to lactaldehyde with hydrolytic release of ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the strongest dedicated precursor step for threonine: Ritson & Sutherland reported lactaldehyde in 19% yield from lactonitrile under Cu(I)/H2S/UV conditions, explicitly identifying it as the Strecker precursor of (allo-)threonine. The balanced sulfur bookkeeping is made explicit here to avoid the mass-balance failures of earlier versions (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker formation of a threonine aminonitrile pool\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated HCN/NH3 pool; near-neutral to mildly alkaline; non-stereoselective aminonitrile formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lactaldehyde condenses with ammonia to an imine/iminium that is trapped by cyanide, yielding 2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson & Sutherland state that added HCN and NH3 convert lactaldehyde smoothly to the (allo)threonine aminonitrile, and Patel et al. likewise describe threonine aminonitrile as an inevitable by-product of cyanosulfidic chemistry. Because the aminonitrile yield and threo:allo ratio were not reported, the node is represented as a stereochemically unresolved aminonitrile pool (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of threonine aminonitrile to threonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool prebiotic pool or geothermal cycling; effective stoichiometry is aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> threonine + NH3.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis converts the aminonitrile into the free amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This hydrolysis is chemically required but experimentally unresolved for threonine in the cited cyanosulfidic literature, which stops at the aminonitrile stage; it is therefore partly speculative. It is placed in cool prebiotic pools because high-temperature residence rapidly destroys threonine, making hydrothermal accumulation implausible (Patel et al., 2015; Aubrey et al., 2009; Higgs & Pudritz, 2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of threonine aminonitrile to allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-335K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cool prebiotic pool or geothermal cycling; effective stoichiometry is aminonitrile + 2 H2O -> allothreonine + NH3.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same nitrile-hydrolysis sequence yields the allo diastereomer from the nonselective aminonitrile pool.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nonenzymatic threonine chemistry is expected to be diastereomerically mixed. Trace spark residues showed roughly equal threonine and allothreonine, so an allothreonine hydrolysis branch should accompany any unresolved aminonitrile pool; keeping it explicit is more faithful to the literature than forcing a single threonine product (Ring et al., 1972 as compiled by Weber & Miller, 1981; Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted glycine-acetaldehyde aldol addition to threonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(II)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated mildly basic pool or mineral interface, often carbonate/bicarbonate buffered; Cu-glycinate activation; reversible retro-aldol chemistry remains a sink.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu-bound glycine acts as an enolate equivalent and adds to acetaldehyde, giving the beta-hydroxy alpha-amino acid after proton transfer.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the only intentionally designed route that has actually produced free threonine itself rather than merely a precursor. Sato, Okawa and Akabori obtained threonine plus allothreonine from copper glycinate, and later work confirmed the strong sensitivity of yield and diastereomer ratio to catalyst and base. The route is chemically strong but geochemically weak because it relies on concentrated Cu(II) chemistry and is reversible (Sato et al., 1957; Ikutani et al., 1959).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted glycine-acetaldehyde aldol addition to allothreonine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(II)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(II)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated mildly basic pool or mineral interface, often carbonate/bicarbonate buffered; Cu-glycinate activation; product ratio depends strongly on base and residence time.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same Cu-assisted aldol addition yields the allo diastereomer when the new stereocenters form with the alternative relative configuration.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Akabori-type chemistry is inherently diastereomeric. The literature reports both threonine and allothreonine, with threo:allo varying widely with catalyst loading, base strength and time. Explicitly including the allothreonine branch is therefore necessary for a faithful threonine network rather than an artificially selective one (Sato et al., 1957; Ikutani et al., 1959; Weber & Miller, 1981).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Full cyanosulfidic Strecker sequence from formaldehyde\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde is trapped as glycolonitrile, reduced through glycolaldehyde and acetaldehyde to lactonitrile and lactaldehyde, then converted through a nonselective aminonitrile pool to threonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile-entry Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry variant that assumes glycolonitrile is supplied by other HCN chemistry before the same surface sequence to threonine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde-entry Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry at the C2 sugar stage; only acetaldehyde generation, lactonitrile/lactaldehyde formation and Strecker-hydrolysis remain.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetaldehyde-entry Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry variant in which acetaldehyde is already available from independent surface photochemistry or discharge chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactonitrile-entry Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Shortest dedicated precursor sequence that begins at the acetaldehyde cyanohydrin and exploits the experimentally supported photoreduction to lactaldehyde.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Lactaldehyde-entry Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Most compressed dedicated route; assumes the Strecker aldehyde for threonine is already present in a cool cyanide-ammonia pool.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Full Akabori-type aldol route from formaldehyde plus glycine\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde is upgraded through the cyanosulfidic branch to acetaldehyde, which is then captured by independently supplied glycine in a Cu-assisted aldol reaction.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile-entry aldol route\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry variant in which glycolonitrile and glycine are already available; only acetaldehyde generation and the final aldol capture remain.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde-entry aldol route\",\n      \"description\": \"Entry at glycolaldehyde with glycine supplied independently; this isolates acetaldehyde production as the last upstream bottleneck before aldol capture.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Minimal glycine-acetaldehyde aldol route\",\n      \"description\": \"Direct Cu-assisted condensation of pre-existing glycine and acetaldehyde in a concentrated mildly basic prebiotic pool.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Threonine", "overall_strategy": "Threonine is treated as a late, low-flux product that should accumulate only in cool surface or prebiotic-pool settings. The network therefore uses the two best-supported threonine chemistries from the literature: a cyanosulfidic surface sequence that upgrades formaldehyde and HCN to lactaldehyde and then to a nonselective threonine aminonitrile pool, and a separate Cu-assisted aldol capture of independently available glycine by acetaldehyde. Ten pathways are represented as distinct precursor-entry routes through these two connected modules; allothreonine is kept explicit, while productive hydrothermal threonine synthesis is omitted because current evidence for it is poor and threonine is thermally fragile at high temperature.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "S", "iupac_name": "sulfur", "common_name": "elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H3NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyacetonitrile", "common_name": "glycolonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H5NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanenitrile", "common_name": "lactonitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C3H6O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypropanal", "common_name": "lactaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H8N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanenitrile", "common_name": "threonine/allothreonine aminonitrile pool", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C4H9NO3", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-hydroxybutanoic acid (threo diastereomer)", "common_name": "threonine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"i