 but chemically standard abstraction. The cited cyanosulfidic literature reaches the same glycolonitrile node by longer HCN homologation chemistry, whereas direct formaldehyde cyanohydrin capture is used here to expose the threonine-relevant network without adding unrelated upstream steps (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cu-sulfide photoreduction of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuCN"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "CuCN", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-irradiated sulfidic pool, phosphate-buffered near pH 7; elemental sulfur is a bookkeeping sink for oxidized sulfur"}, "mechanism": "UV-driven Cu-mediated sulfide photoredox reduces the nitrile carbon to an aldehyde equivalent and hydrolyzes the reduced intermediate, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Ritson and Sutherland showed CuCN/phosphate/HS- plus UV can reduce nitrile-derived intermediates toward aldehydes. This net step captures glycolonitrile-to-glycolaldehyde conversion; sulfur is shown as elemental S only as a bookkeeping sink because the true oxidized sulfur pool is more complex. Sulfide supply and UV attenuation remain important geochemical constraints (Ritson & Sutherland, 2013; Patel et al., 2015; Ranjan et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cu-sulfide photoreduction of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuCN"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "CuCN", "aqueous": true, "other": "same UV/sulfidic surface setting as rxn_002; phosphate-buffered; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Related Cu-sulfide photoredox reduces and deoxygenates glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde, with sulfide serving as reductant and sulfur as the bookkeeping oxidat